    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0d097edd_aa7c71c8",
        "filename": "authfs/src/fsverity/metadata/metadata.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 65,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-12T10:45:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do we know that sig_size and signature are all zeros in this copy?\n(Just looking at http://cs/android/external/kernel-headers/original/uapi/linux/fsverity.h?l\u003d43\u0026rcl\u003d11249cacaf0d97f5b752a60e59c022a7322f370a)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 65,
        "startChar": 25,
        "endLine": 65,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "41cecbbeaea4d640c74834bc54c30aaa902f1d0e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2d3f949b_33eedbd6",
        "filename": "authfs/src/fsverity/metadata/metadata.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 65,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042276
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-12T20:18:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes. For regular fs-verity implementation, the descriptor is typically stored on disk for kernel (the specific filesystem) to use.  In the design, the signature is stored in the same struct, but the value (either during calculation by fsverity-util, or returned from the kernel through ioctl) is zero(d).",
      "parentUuid": "0d097edd_aa7c71c8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 65,
        "startChar": 25,
        "endLine": 65,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "41cecbbeaea4d640c74834bc54c30aaa902f1d0e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0f757948_c05da4d9",
        "filename": "authfs/src/fsverity/metadata/metadata.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 68,
      "author": {
        "id": 1291338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-12T03:54:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s unlikely, but this can cause a problem on a big endian device, because multibyte fields in self.header.descriptor are converted from little endian, not as-is. (See line 107)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 63,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "41cecbbeaea4d640c74834bc54c30aaa902f1d0e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3c91a6b9_3016a707",
        "filename": "authfs/src/fsverity/metadata/metadata.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 68,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-12T10:45:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think that\u0027s ok here - we\u0027re just hashing the bytes, and the hash is intended to be on the data in little-endian form. (The reason it specifies little-endian is to ensure the hashes are consistent.)",
      "parentUuid": "0f757948_c05da4d9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 63,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "41cecbbeaea4d640c74834bc54c30aaa902f1d0e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "320f4742_1da30f20",
        "filename": "authfs/src/fsverity/metadata/metadata.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 68,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042276
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-12T20:18:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Right, this is working as intended.  Regardless of the device endian-ness, the hash calculation always uses little-endian. Think of enabling fs-verity on ext4 for an portable storage. It must work across BE/LE devices.",
      "parentUuid": "3c91a6b9_3016a707",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 63,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "41cecbbeaea4d640c74834bc54c30aaa902f1d0e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b9bfbb95_2c140c2a",
        "filename": "authfs/src/fsverity/metadata/metadata.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 68,
      "author": {
        "id": 1291338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-13T00:07:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Nope, fields in this header are represented as native endian, not a little endian. FSVerityMetadata is parsed with parse_fsverity_metadata, and it converts related fields from little endian to device endian (by u32::from_le, u64::from_le). This function is called after endian conversion, so it\u0027s not guaranteed to be little endian.\n\nIs it the case that Rust always uses little endian?",
      "parentUuid": "320f4742_1da30f20",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 63,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "41cecbbeaea4d640c74834bc54c30aaa902f1d0e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1a8378d2_6e8b5102",
        "filename": "authfs/src/fsverity/metadata/metadata.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 68,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042276
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-13T00:22:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah yes, good point.  Then I think the digest needs to be calculated during the parsing, when the raw data is still available.  I\u0027ll re-work.\n\n(And I don\u0027t think Rust handles endian implicitly, or should any programming languages do)",
      "parentUuid": "b9bfbb95_2c140c2a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 63,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "41cecbbeaea4d640c74834bc54c30aaa902f1d0e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
